id: DRAFT-musl-rwlock-writer-starvation
severity: 2
category: ohos|libc|threading
trigger_tool: conan
phase: test
approach: exclude-test-with-documented-reason

keywords: pthread_rwlock, writer starvation, rwlock deadlock, testrwlock, Timeout, musl, rwlock

error_signature: testrwlock.*(Timeout|deadlock)|rwlock.*writer.*(starve|hang|deadlock)|pthread_rwlock_wrlock.*hang
match_keywords: pthread_rwlock, writer starvation, rwlock deadlock, testrwlock, Timeout, musl

error_excerpt: Test #11: testrwlock ....***Timeout 20.06 sec (writer thread never acquires the lock; readers loop forever)

root_cause: OHOS musl pthread_rwlock has no writer-queue fairness: a waiting writer does not block new readers, so under a saturated reader pattern (SDL testrwlock: 6 readers holding the lock 1000ms with 10ms gaps) the writer starves forever. glibc/macOS/Windows rwlocks all provide writer relief. This is a libc semantics difference, not a logic defect in the library under test. Verified experimentally: a bare pthread_rwlock probe acquires the write lock normally when unsaturated, and testrwlock --nbthreads 1 passes.
affected_scope: any headless test that runs saturated readers + a writer against pthread_rwlock on OHOS

rec_priority: 1
rec_approach: exclude-test-with-documented-reason
rec_description: Distinguish platform difference from real defect with two experiments: (1) a bare pthread_rwlock probe (writers+readers) acquires the write lock normally when unsaturated; (2) the failing test with reduced contention (e.g. --nbthreads 1) passes. If both hold, exclude the specific test from the headless CTest run with ctest -E and document the technical reason (APIs like SDL_RWLock do not promise writer priority).
rec_command: ctest --output-on-failure --timeout 180 -E '^testrwlock$'
kb_ref: E267

prescan_pattern: pthread_rwlock
last_updated: 2026-09-20
